## a b s t r a c t
Developing metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) derived microwave absorbers with the merits of thin matching thickness, broad bandwidth and strong absorption still remains a big challenge in the electromagnetic absorption field. Herein, FeNi-MOFs derived magnetic-carbon composites were fabricated via a solvothermal and pyrolytic two-step strategy. It was found that the micromorphology of carbon frameworks could be regulated from the regular octahedron to spherical shape through facilely adjusting the molar ratios of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ to $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ in the precursors. Furthermore, results revealed that the molar ratios of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ to $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ had notable effects on the electromagnetic parameters and microwave attenuation capacity of attained composites. Significantly, the obtained $\mathrm{FeNi}/C$ composite with the molar ratio of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ to $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ of 1:0.5 showed the comprehensively optimal electromagnetic attenuation performance, i.e. the reflection loss achieved 40.2 dB (larger than $99.99\%$ absorption) and absorption frequency band was as high as $5.8\mathsf{G H z}$ (from 11.9 to $17.7\:\mathrm{GHz}$ , covering $96.7\%$ of Ku-band) under an ultrathin thickness of $1.65~\mathrm{{mm}}$ Besides, the probable microwave dissipation mechanisms were clarified, which mainly derived from the optimized impedance matching, strengthened interfacial polarization and dipole polarization relaxation, enhanced conduction loss and natural resonance effect. Therefore, our results would be helpful for designing and developing high-performance microwave absorbing composites derived from MOFs.
