## 1. Introduction
## 2. Experimental
With the development of communication technology and extensive usage of electronic devices, all kinds of electronic devices bring convenience to people’s life, but also cause potential hazard on human health [1,2]. In order to deal with the growing serious electromagnetic pollution problem, it is urgent to develop highefficiency microwave absorbers with low reflection loss, broad bandwidth and thin matching thickness [3–6].
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a new class of threedimensional (3D) porous materials, which are composed of inorganic metal centers and organic ligands [7,8]. MOFs possess the adjustable chemical structure and micromorphology, large specific surface area and well-developed porosity [7,8], which show good application prospects in energy storage and conversion, catalysts, adsorbents and other fields [9–13].
Recently, MOFs derived carbon-based composites have been regarded as a novel type of lightweight and high-efficiency electromagnetic absorbents [14–16]. Especially, magnetic metal Fe or Nibased MOFs derived magnetic-carbon composites have shown great potentials for microwave absorption application owing to their facile preparation, low cost, adjustable morphology and magnetoelectric synergistic loss mechanism [17–30]. For example, Xiang et al. fabricated ferroferric oxide@nanoporous carbon $(\mathrm{Fe}_{3^{-}}$ $\begin{array}{r}{\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{N P C})}\end{array}$ composites with good microwave dissipation performance, i.e. the minimum reflection loss $(R L_{\mathrm{min}})$ reached $-65.5$ dB and effective absorption bandwidth (EAB) achieved $4.5\:\mathrm{GHz}$ under a matching thickness $(d)$ of $3.0\mathrm{mm}$ [20]. Wu et al. synthesized the magnetic NPC nanorods derived from Fe-MIL-88A with a $R L_{\mathrm{min}}$ of $-52.9$ dB and EAB of $4.64~\mathrm{GHz}$ at a thick thickness of $3.07~\mathrm{{mm}}$ [21]. Che et al. prepared the ${\mathsf{N i@C}}$ composites through carbonizing the Ni-MOFs. The attained composites possessed an optimal $R L_{\mathrm{min}}$ of $-46.9$ dB at a thick thickness of $3.5\mathrm{mm}$ [30]. Nevertheless, most of reported Fe or Ni-based MOFs derived magnetic-carbon composites absorbents have some drawbacks such as narrow frequency band $({\mathrm{EAB}}\leq5.5\ {\mathrm{GHz}})$ and thick thickness $\cdot d>2.0\ \mathrm{mm}$ . Therefore, the development of high-efficiency microwave absorbers derived from Fe or Ni-based MOFs that simultaneously meet the requirements of strong absorption, broad band and thin thickness still remains a major challenge in the electromagnetic absorption field. Herein, iron nickel metal–organic frameworks (FeNi-MOFs) derived magnetic-carbon $(\gamma{-}\mathrm{Fe_{2}O_{3}}/\mathrm{C},\ \mathrm{FeNi}/\mathrm{C},\ \mathrm{FeNi_{3}}/\mathrm{C}$ and $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf C)$ composites were fabricated by a solvothermal and pyrolytic twostep route. The crystal structure, degree of graphitization, surface composition, morphology, magnetic properties, electromagnetic parameters and microwave attenuation capacity of as-prepared composites were carefully examined by various characterization techniques. It was observed that the micromorphology of attained composites could be modulated through facilely regulating the molar ratios of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ to $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ in the FeNi-MOFs precursors. Moreover, the as-fabricated composites presented synthetically excellent electromagnetic dissipation capacity with the advantages of robust attenuation, wide frequency band and thin thickness. Besides, the probable microwave dissipation mechanisms were clarified.
The fabrication processes of $\mathrm{FeNi}/C$ composite are schematically illustrated in Fig. 1. Firstly, metal salts $\mathrm{(FeCl_{3}.6H_{2}O}$ and $\mathsf{N i C l}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0\rangle$ 1 and organic ligand (p-phthalic acid, $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ ) were added into the solvent of N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF). Then, the mixtures reacted under the solvothermal condition at $110.0\ ^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ for $24.0\mathrm{~h~}$ . Lastly, the obtained FeNi-MOFs precursor were transformed into FeNi/C composite by a simple pyrolysis treatment at $800.0~^{\circ}C$ for $2.0\mathrm{{h}}$ under argon atmosphere.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Structural investigation
The crystalline structure of attained binary composites was studied by X-ray diffraction (XRD). As described in Fig. 2, the diffraction peaks at $2\theta=35.7$ , 57.5 and $63.1^{\circ}$ in the S1 should be assigned to the (119), (1115) and (4012) crystal planes of $\gamma-$ $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ (JCPDS No. 25-1402), separately [31]. The peaks at 43.7, 50.8 and $74.7^{\circ}$ in the S2 are indexed to the (111), (200) and (220) planes of FeNi (JCPDS No. 47-1417), respectively [24]. The three peaks of S3 at $2\theta=44.3\$ , 51.6 and $75.8^{\circ}$ are attributed to the (111), (200) and (220) planes of $\mathrm{FeNi}_{3}$ (JCPDS No. 38-0419), separately [24]. In addition, the three sharp peaks of S4 at $2\theta\:=\:44.6$ , 52.0 and $76.5^{\circ}$ are ascribed to the (111), (200) and (220) planes of metallic Ni (JCPDS No. 04-0850), respectively [32]. Nevertheless, the diffraction peaks of C are hard to distinguish in all samples. This phenomenon may be explained by the low diffraction strength or amorphous structure of C in the samples. Thus, the results of XRD analysis manifest that the composites gradually change from $\gamma{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}0_{3}/\mathrm{C}$ to $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf C$ with the increasing of the molar ratios of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ to $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ .
Normally, the degree of graphitization has a notable influence on the complex permittivity and dielectric loss of microwave absorbents [33], which can be evaluated by Raman spectra. As presented in Fig. 3(a), the Raman scattering peaks at $1580~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1344~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are named as the G band and D band, separately [34,35]. In general, the G band denotes the $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ hybridization, while the D band represents the disorder or $\mathsf{s p}^{3}$ defects [34,35]. The value of $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ (strength ratio of D band to G band) is usually used to assess the degree of disorder or defects. The calculated values of $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ for S4, S3, S2 and S1 are 0.94, 0.94, 0.95 and 0.93, respectively. Therefore, the molar ratios of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ to $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ have little influence on the degree of graphitization of as-fabricated composites. The surface composition and elemental valences of S2 were analyzed through $\mathsf{X}$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). The wide scan (Fig. 3(b)) verifies the presence of Ni, Fe, O and C elements. As presented in Fig. 3(c), the C 1s spectrum is divided into three peaks that are $\scriptstyle0-{\mathsf{C}}=0$ , CAO and ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{=}}{\mathsf{C}}$ [36]. From Fig. 3(d), the O 1s spectrum is fitted into three peaks, i.e. CAO, $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ and metal oxides [22]. It should be noted that the appearance of metal oxides indicates the surface oxidization of metallic Fe or Ni [37]. Fig. 3(e) describes the spectrum of Fe 2p, the notable peaks appear at 711.1 and $723.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ , matching to the $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ , the peaks at 713.6 and $726.4\mathrm{eV}$ manifest the presence of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ , and the peak at 732.0 verify the presence of satellite peak [22,37]. Furthermore, the peaks at 708.7 and $719.3\mathrm{eV}$ indicate the presence of Fe [22,37]. As depicted in Fig. 3(f), the peaks at 853.3 and $873.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ correspond to Ni. The peak at $855.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ manifests the presence of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ , which could be induced by the surface oxidization of Ni. In addition, the peaks at 861.6 and $880.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ correspond to the satellite peaks [32].
## 3.2. Morphological analysis
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) is a powerful tool to detect the micromorphology of composites. As presented in Fig. 4(a)–(c), it is obvious that the morphology of carbon frame is a regular octahedron with almost the uniform size distribution. As illustrated in Fig. 4(d)–(l), with further increasing the molar ratios of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ to $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ , the morphology of as-prepared composites gradually changes from regular octahedron to irregular polyhedron, and finally tends to be spherical shape. It can be found that the molar ratios of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ to $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ have a great influence on the morphology of carbon frameworks. Fig. $4(\mathrm{m})$ schematically describes the morphology evolution of carbon frameworks from the regular octahedron to spherical shape in the attained composites by simply adjusting the molar ratios of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ to $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ .
Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) was also utilized to examine the micromorphology of attained composite (S2). As described in Fig. 5(a), the carbon skeleton with octahedra shape can be clearly observed. From Fig. 5(b), the high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) image reveals the interplanar spacing of $0.209\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which is in accordance with the (111) plane of FeNi alloy [24]. The distribution of elements in the S2 was analyzed via energy dispersive spectrometer (EDS) mapping, as displayed in Fig. 5(c)–(f). Results reveal that the O, Fe and Ni elements are primarily distributed on the carbon frameworks, while the C element is almost uniformly dispersed in the total region.
## 3.3. Magnetic properties
Normally, the magnetic loss is crucial for microwave attenuation of magnetic absorbents [38]. Fig. 6 shows the typical magnetization versus magnetic field strength $(M\sim H)$ curves of S1–S4. Interestingly, all samples exhibit a representative ferromagnetic hysteresis characteristic. Furthermore, the saturation magnetization $(M_{s})$ values of S1–S4 are 87.3, 63.7, 60.2 and $48.9\mathrm{\emu/g}$ , separately. Thus, the $M_{s}$ values decline with the decreasing of the molar ratios of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ to $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ in the precursors.
## 3.4. Microwave dissipation properties
The microwave dissipation capability of absorbents is closely related to their electromagnetic parameters [39–41]. Generally, the real permittivity $\mathbf{\eta}(\varepsilon^{\prime})$ and real permeability $(\mu^{\prime})$ represent the storage capacity of electric and magnetic field energy, whereas the imaginary permittivity $(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime})$ and imaginary permeability $(\mu^{\prime\prime})$ indicate the dissipation capacity of electric and magnetic field energy, respectively [39–41]. From Fig. 7(a) and (b), the $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ declines with the decreasing of molar ratios of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ to $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ , and the $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ possesses the same trend as $\mathbf{\chi}_{\varepsilon}^{\mathbf{\sigma}}$ . Furthermore, the $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ of S1–S4 shows a decline trend in the range of $2.0{-}13.0\operatorname{GHz}$ and obvious fluctuations during $13.0{-}18.0\mathrm{GHz}$ . Notably, there are some relaxation peaks in Fig. 7(a) (marked by the red dotted boxes), which manifests the existence of multiple polarization relaxation [42]. Fig. 7(c) and (d) describe the variation of $\mu^{\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ with increasing the frequency. Specifically, the $\mu^{\prime}$ and $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ values for S1–S4 are fluctuated between 0.9 and 1.67 and 0–0.45. For the sake of evaluating the dielectric loss capacity, the $\tan\delta_{\mathrm{e}}$ versus $f$ curves were drawn (Fig. 7(e)). The $\tan\delta_{\mathrm{e}}$ values generally decline in the sequence of ${\mathsf{S}}1\geq{\mathsf{S}}2>{\mathsf{S}}3\geq{\mathsf{S}}4$ , corresponding to the variation trend of $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ and $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ . The $\tan\delta_{\mathrm{{m}}}$ versus $f$ curves were plotted for assessing the magnetic loss characteristic. As we expected, the $\tan\delta_{\mathrm{{m}}}$ versus $f$ curves of the four specimens in Fig. 7(f) show analogical variation trends as $\mu^{\prime\prime}$ .
To examine the influence of molar ratios of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ to $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ on the electromagnetic attenuation capability of attained composites, the two-dimensional (2D) and 3D RL curves of S1–S4 were drawn. From Fig. 8(a)– $\cdot({\mathsf{d}}^{\prime})$ , the $R L_{\mathrm{min}}$ values from S1 to S4 are $-42.3$ dB $(1.5~\mathrm{mm})$ , $-43.0$ dB ( $1.59~\mathrm{mm}$ ), $-50.3$ dB $(3.0~\mathrm{mm})$ and $-6.9$ dB $(4.0\ \mathrm{mm})$ , respectively. Meanwhile, the values of EAB from S1 to S4 are $4.0\mathsf{G H z}$ $(2.0~\mathrm{mm})$ ), $5.8~\mathrm{GHz}$ ( $1.65~\mathrm{mm}^{\cdot}$ ), $4.6\mathsf{G H z}$ ( $1.67~\mathrm{mm};$ 1 and 0 GHz, separately. Fig. 8(e) shows the 3D histogram of EAB for S1–S4. It is obvious that the S2 shows the widest EAB under a very thin thickness of $1.65~\mathrm{{mm}}$ . Considering the demands of thin thickness, wide frequency band and low reflection loss for the practical application of absorbents, the S2 presents the comprehensively optimal electromagnetic dissipation performance among the four samples.
In general, the eddy current loss and magnetic resonance loss including natural resonance and exchange resonance are mainly responsible for magnetic loss in the GHz frequency band [34,43]. The eddy current loss can be analyzed via the following formulas [34,43]:
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{\mu^{\prime\prime}\approx2\pi\mu_{0}(\mu^{\prime})^{2}\sigma d^{2}f/3}}\\ {{\ }}\\ {{C_{0}=\mu^{\prime\prime}(\mu^{\prime})^{-2}f^{-1}}}\end{array}
$$
where $\mu_{0}$ represents the vacuum permeability, and $\sigma$ signifies the electrical conductivity. Normally, the eddy current coefficient $(C_{0})$ will keep constant if the magnetic loss primarily originates from eddy current [34,43]. As presented in Fig. 9(a), the $C_{0}$ generally declines when the frequency increases. As a consequence, the eddy current loss should be excluded for magnetic loss. Additionally, the notable peaks in $C_{0}\sim f$ curves at 3.0–6.0 GHz should be attributed to the natural resonance [34,43].
It is widely accepted that the microwave dissipation capacity of materials is basically determined by the impedance matching and electromagnetic loss characteristics [44,45]. $\vert Z_{\mathrm{in}}/Z_{0}\vert$ , i.e. the modulus of normalized input impedance is utilized to evaluate the impedance matching condition, which is calculated as follows [46–48]:
$$
\left|\frac{Z_{\mathrm{in}}}{Z_{0}}\right|=\left|\sqrt{\frac{\mu_{\mathrm{r}}}{\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}}}\mathrm{tanh}\left[j\left(\frac{2\pi f d}{c}\right)\sqrt{\mu_{\mathrm{r}}\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}}\right]\right|
$$
Herein $Z_{0}$ denotes the air impedance, and $Z_{\mathrm{in}}$ signifies the input impedance [46–48].
Attenuation constant $(\alpha)$ is often used to reflect the electromagnetic loss capability, which can be expressed as follows [34,43]:
$$
\alpha=\frac{\sqrt{2}\pi f}{c}\times\sqrt{\left(\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}-\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime}\right)+\sqrt{\left(\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}-\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime}\right)^{2}+\left(\varepsilon^{\prime}\mu^{\prime\prime}+\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\mu^{\prime}\right)^{2}}}
$$
Fig. 9(b) reveals that the S1 has much larger $\alpha$ values than other three specimens almost in the whole measured frequency range, suggesting the strengthened attenuation loss capacity. Fig. 9(c)
depicts the $\vert Z_{\mathrm{in}}/Z_{0}\vert$ versus $f$ curves. Obviously, the $\vert Z_{\mathrm{in}}/Z_{0}\vert$ values stay away from 1.0 for S3 and S4, suggesting a bad impedance matching. Nevertheless, the $\vert Z_{\mathrm{in}}/Z_{0}\vert$ of S2 is nearly equal to 1.0 at $12.2\ \mathrm{GHz}$ . The S3 and S4 present the relatively weak attenuation ability and bad impedance matching, which result in poor microwave dissipation performance. Although the attenuation ability of S1 is larger than S2, its impedance mismatch causes poor microwave dissipation. Thus, the S2 shows the strongest microwave dissipation properties chiefly coming from the optimal impedance matching and moderate electromagnetic attenuation.
In view of the quarter wavelength $(\lambda/4)$ model, when the thickness of absorbers $(t_{\mathrm{m}})$ and peak frequency $(f_{\mathrm{m}})$ meet the following formula [34,43]:
$$
t_{m}={\frac{n\lambda}{4}}={\frac{n c}{4f_{m}\sqrt{\left|\varepsilon_{r}\mu_{r}\right|}}}(n=1,3,5,\ldots)
$$
A phase cancellation effect should appear that could availably dissipate the incident microwaves [34,43].
Fig. 10 displays the typical reflection loss (RL), $t_{\mathrm{m}}$ and $\vert Z_{\mathrm{in}}/Z_{0}\vert$ versus $f$ curves of S2. The blue sphere represents the experimental thickness $(t_{\mathrm{m}}^{\mathrm{exp}})$ , while the red dash curve signifies the simulation thickness $(t_{\mathrm{m}}^{\mathrm{sim}})$ . Impressively, as the $R L_{\mathrm{min}}$ $(-43.0~\mathrm{dB})$ is achieved, the impedance matching is equal to 1.0, and the $t_{\mathrm{m}}^{\mathrm{exp}}$ exactly falls on the $\lambda/4$ curve. Therefore, the $\lambda/4$ rule should be taken into consideration for the design of microwave absorbents.
Fig. 11 illustrates the probable electromagnetic dissipation mechanisms of $\mathrm{FeNi}/C$ composite. We assume that the microwaves are incident normally from the front of absorber $({\mathrm{FeNi}}/C$ composite) and are backed by a perfect conductor (metal plate) [34]. The underlying microwave absorption mechanisms of $\mathrm{FeNi}/C$ composite could be ascribed to the following aspects. Firstly, there are some structure defects in the carbon matrix, which can act as the polarization centers and further induce the dipole polarization under the alternating electromagnetic field that could dissipate the incident microwaves [22,49]. Secondly, abundantly heterogeneous interfaces between FeNi alloy and carbon matrix can be considered as the capacitor-like structure [22,49]. According to the model proposed by Cao et al., the capacitor-like structure at the interfaces could attenuate the power of incident microwaves by aligning the polar bonds or charges under the alternating electromagnetic fields [43,50]. Thirdly, from electron hopping theory [51–53], electrons can absorb the electromagnetic energy and then migrate on the carbon matrix and FeNi alloy, inducing the conduction loss and further converting the electromagnetic energy into thermal energy by colliding with the lattice [22,54,55]. Lastly, the synergistic effects of dielectric loss derived from interfacial polarization and dipole polarization, magnetic resonance loss originated from ferromagnetic FeNi alloy, and conduction loss come from graphite carbon and FeNi alloy significantly enhance the microwave attenuation and optimize the impedance matching.
Microwave absorption capacity of attained $\mathrm{FeNi}/C$ composite (S2) has been compared with other Fe and Ni-based MOFs derived magnetic-carbon composites reported in the previous studies [17– 30]. Considering the demands of strong absorption and thin thickness, the specific minimum reflection loss per thickness $(S R L_{\mathrm{min}})$ is proposed to evaluate the integrated electromagnetic dissipation capacity [56]. As presented in Table 1, the S2 in this work shows the largest $\left|S R L_{\mathrm{min}}\right|$ of $27.0~\mathrm{dB/mm}$ and broad EAB of $5.8~\mathrm{GHz}$ at $1.65\mathrm{mm}$ . Therefore, the as-fabricated FeNi/C composite in the present study display tremendous potential for the practical application as a high-efficiency microwave absorbent.
## 4. Conclusions
In conclusion, FeNi-MOFs derived magnetic-carbon composites were fabricated via solvothermal and high-temperature pyrolysis processes. Results demonstrated that the micromorphology of carbon frameworks changed from regular octahedron to irregular polyhedron, and finally tended to be spherical shape through simply adjusting the molar ratios of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ to $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ in the precursors. Furthermore, it could be observed that the molar ratios of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ to $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ had a significant influence on the crystalline structure, static magnetic properties and electromagnetic dissipation capacity of asfabricated composites. Significantly, the attained FeNi/C composite with the molar ratio of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ to $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ of 1:0.5 showed the synthetically optimal electromagnetic attenuation performance, which simultaneously satisfied the requirements of thin thickness, broad bandwidth and strong absorption. Besides, the potential electromagnetic dissipation mechanisms were proposed. Therefore, this work put forward a valuable strategy for designing of Fe or Nibased MOFs derived magnetic-carbon composites as highefficiency electromagnetic absorbents.
